{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-evidence-processing", "file": "references/repeating-separator-tiles.md", "content": "# Repeating tiles across separator lines: task-901d0831

Verified task rule: a full line of color 6 separates rectangular regions; one small tile repeats across each adjacent region while the separator remains intact. The demos disambiguated the axis: in demo 1 a vertical separator at column 4 divided a 4-column tile from a 4-column region; the tile pattern replicated to the right. In demo 2 a horizontal separator at row 3 divided a 3-row tile from a 3-row region; the top tile replicated downward. The test combined both: a 3x6 tile at upper-left, a full vertical separator column at index 6 and full horizontal separator rows at indices 3 and 7. Replicate tile values across and down the non-separator regions; retain the separator lines as color 6. The resulting 11x13 submission was confirmed correct.

Generalizable check: determine tile dimensions as the distance from grid boundary to separator; tile each region using corresponding tile coordinates. Verify all dimensions and preserve separator cells. Don't infer replication from only one separator orientation when both dimensions or irregular region sizes could support alternative rules.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545321/attempts/695f0949c61e4d9285c5659d167e433e/system/state/hermes/skills/arc/arc-demo-evidence-processing/references/repeating-separator-tiles.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-synthesis", "file": "references/demo-synthesis-serial-request-lesson.md", "content": "# Demo integration before requesting again

In a session with several costly demos, the main operational failure was requesting the next example immediately after receiving the previous one, without stating its constraint or comparing it to the live hypotheses. The later examples were rich and heterogeneous; proper synthesis should have extracted their changed/unchanged cells and cross-compared them before another request.

For every returned pair, write down what it proves and what remains ambiguous. Request another only when you can name the competing rules and how the possible pair outcomes would change the test prediction. Do not treat request availability or uncertainty as sufficient justification. This complements the general demo synthesis workflow; it does not establish a transformation rule for this task, whose demos in this conversation did not resolve the input motif.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545321/attempts/695f0949c61e4d9285c5659d167e433e/system/state/hermes/skills/arc/arc-demo-synthesis/references/demo-synthesis-serial-request-lesson.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-evidence-processing", "file": "references/tile-statistics-analysis.md", "content": "# Tile statistics for compact ARC outputs

When a large grid is partitioned into equal tiles and reduced to a smaller output, compare each output cell against the full tile rather than testing only a favorite sample coordinate. Record the histogram and test mode, presence/absence, dominant non-background value, and positional candidates. Include ties and tiles whose most frequent value is the apparent background. Validate the candidate cellwise across the complete demo before applying it; request another example if unresolved tie or selection behavior could alter the test.

In task-197199f9 demo 1 (18x18 to 6x6, 3x3 tiles), every output value equals its tile's mode. This is a useful candidate, not yet a verified universal rule: one demo cannot establish tie-breaking or rule out another statistic that coincides on these tiles.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545321/attempts/695f0949c61e4d9285c5659d167e433e/system/state/hermes/skills/arc/arc-demo-evidence-processing/references/tile-statistics-analysis.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-evidence-processing", "file": "references/compact-output-after-cap.md", "content": "# Compact output after exhausting demonstrations

For large, regular inputs that map to small outputs, do not guess a panel mask, center sample, or crop from dimensions alone. Enumerate plausible source coordinates (periodic row/column indices, intersections, exceptional bands) and align each output cell against the demo. Record source-value tuples and output values, including counterexamples where candidate coordinates agree but outputs differ. Validate every output cell in every demo before applying a mapping.

After a rejected submission, discard the whole candidate; do not make a speculative near-variant. If the demo cap is exhausted, revisit output dimensions and transformation class rather than treating the remaining guesses as justified. Submit only a candidate supported by the complete demo set; preserve one-action JSON protocol.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545321/attempts/695f0949c61e4d9285c5659d167e433e/system/state/hermes/skills/arc/arc-demo-evidence-processing/references/compact-output-after-cap.md"}
